1 NAME Thure Ludvig Theodor /Kumlien/
2 GIVN Thure Ludvig Theodor
2 SURN Kumlien
2 _MARNM Kumlien
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Sweet Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Albion
3 STAE Wisconsin
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 9 NOV 1819
2 PLAC Härlunda församling
2 ADDR
3 CITY Skara
3 STAE Västra Götalands län
3 CTRY Sweden
1 DEAT
2 DATE 5 AUG 1888
2 ADDR
3 CITY Milwaukee
3 STAE Wisconsin
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Farmer, Ornithologist, Naturalist, Taxidermist
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Burial record:
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/16722755/thure-l_-kumlien
2 CONT
2 CONT Thure Ludwig Theodor Kumlien (November 9, 1819 – August 5, 1888) was a Swedish-American ornithologist, naturalist, and taxidermist. A contemporary of Thoreau, Audubon, and Agassiz, he contributed much to the knowledge of the natur
2 CONC al history of Wisconsin and its birds. He collected and shipped specimens to many investigators in the United States and abroad. He taught botany and zoology, as well as foreign languages, at Albion Academy, and was particularly r
2 CONC egarded as an expert in the identification of birds’ nests.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thure Kumlien was born in 1819 in the parish of Härlunda in Västergötland, Sweden, the oldest of fourteen children in an aristocratic Swedish family. His father, Ludwig Kumlien (1790–1839), was an army quartermaster, and owned sev
2 CONC eral large estates. His mother, Johanna Rhodin (1800–1830) was the daughter of a minister. His early education was with a tutor, after which he entered the Gymnasium of Skara. He subsequently attended Uppsala University, graduatin
2 CONC g in 1843. He took an early interest in natural history and collected many specimens, particularly from the Baltic islands, sending them to Hermann Schlegel of Leiden; Wilhelm Peters of Berlin, Carl Jakob Sundevall of the Swedish 
2 CONC Museum of Natural History, Stockholm, and John Cassin of Boston were among his other correspondents.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kumlien emigrated to the United States in 1843, accompanied by his fiancée, Margaretta Christina Wallberg, and her sister. They first settled in Milwaukee and were married there on September 5 of the same year. Not long afterward,
2 CONC  drawn by letters written by the minister of a local parish, he came to the Lake Koshkonong area of Wisconsin. When he bought his homestead, he bypassed farmland and instead purchased woodland, probably because of his love of natu
2 CONC re, and devoted his spare time to the study of surrounding nature, in particular the local plants, birds, and insects. His first purchase was 40 acres, from the government; he bought another 40 acres later. His first home was a lo
2 CONC g house near Busseyville. Often distracted by local wildlife and plants, Kumlien did not succeed at farming; he supplemented his farm income with taxidermy and by collecting specimens for museums and other scientists.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kumlien's life work constituted a rich personal herbarium and an important collection of birds. He began with a collection acquired by the Boston Society of Natural Sciences in 1854, and expanded his reach to Europe, sending speci
2 CONC mens to such scientists as Elias Magnus Fries of Uppsala and Thomas Mayo Brewer. He also kept up correspondence with Spencer Fullerton Baird, Edward Augustus Samuels, and others. Kumlien was described by no less a luminary than Lo
2 CONC uis Agassiz as the world's foremost authority on the identification of birds' nests. Combined with his modesty, his collecting and correspondence made him more widely known to fellow scientists than to his neighbors. In spite of t
2 CONC he recognition and the regard he received from the scientific community, he lived in tight financial circumstances nearly all of his life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through the efforts of Rasmus Bjørn Anderson, Kumlien accepted a position in the faculty of Albion Academy, in Albion, Wisconsin. From 1867 to 1870, he taught not only botany and zoology, but also foreign languages; he left when f
2 CONC inancial turmoil hit the college. He took a position with the State of Wisconsin in 1870, collecting specimens for the University of Wisconsin in Madison,and the teacher training school (the university’s collections were destroyed
2 CONC  in a fire in 1884). From 1881 to 1883, he worked for the Wisconsin Natural History Society as taxidermist and conservator of its collections.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1883, the Natural History Society's collections were transferred to the Milwaukee Public Museum, and Kumlien began working for the museum in the same capacity, which enabled him to pursue his naturalist studies year round. He b
2 CONC ecame a member of the American Ornithologists' Union the year it was founded (1883). He died in 1888, most likely as a result of exposure to preservatives used on bird specimens sent from South America. He is buried at Sweet Cemet
2 CONC ery, in Albion.[ His work can still be viewed at numerous museums in Europe and America.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bio courtesy of Rusty C. Moe
2 CONT
